#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147345 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "54c15528-aec0-5554-b3b0-f928f3a6d3d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6619" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\nI. $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\nII. $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\nIII. $y'' - 81y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n\n- Equation III does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "4c6cb7ad-3d0d-5d85-bda7-a9c8874ce25b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6620" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 100y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10,\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147347 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "2ca66468-091b-5f69-99eb-2d347486becd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6621" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\nII. $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\nIII. $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "2e1c98d9-bce3-5b6a-bbef-ee32fa2a2448" :question/math-academy-id "e-6622" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 36y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0,\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)},\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6,\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0,$ our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that $y'(0) = 3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/id #uuid "7489abc3-36e8-5c4d-a2cf-a2bcb14b63ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-6623" :question/problem "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\nII. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\nIII. The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nso its equation of motion is \n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\\,R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1}),\\,R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n\n$$\ny(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b31b196-ffe5-5824-b8f3-ccf950676c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330046} {:answer/id #uuid "5eae51ab-60f9-5b49-8f5a-a9fe656ea67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330792} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2939ae-647d-5a92-a35f-64088b11814b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331040} {:answer/id #uuid "83cb5cf0-09f0-5fc0-84ab-5c0e35f6e2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331122} {:answer/id #uuid "98691ed4-494f-57a5-9b29-f6a26a3937ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c2939ae-647d-5a92-a35f-64088b11814b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331040} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c4f606f-21b0-5945-8625-20858994dddc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "916caca2-b090-554c-9711-817f0993a96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64217" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114d6b8f-6471-5003-8bc6-bf96f5a4a260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431123} {:answer/id #uuid "474cbbae-5bb2-50b5-adf4-ee5063eb2c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431127} {:answer/id #uuid "c213ebe9-776a-591e-b982-8a9a80a51a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431132} {:answer/id #uuid "e52d9a63-cb05-593f-8c88-406ceb09fed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431137} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e0dfdc-13d0-53f8-905f-062768c52829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52d9a63-cb05-593f-8c88-406ceb09fed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431137} :answer-field/id #uuid "a959009f-79af-59c7-a873-a9953fa4907f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e5261a8-ad0c-5d41-be10-3bca647fd461" :question/math-academy-id "q-64249" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0035afd4-f801-529f-8c26-e89eab48fce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332989} {:answer/id #uuid "1db218ae-7fc2-5f61-8143-229541181a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333257} {:answer/id #uuid "550f1c38-acd7-5656-af13-aece8cce4084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333765} {:answer/id #uuid "a27ea65a-63dd-575c-8716-61bbc057cee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334417} {:answer/id #uuid "c4eb5222-e0ff-5f2a-86d5-d790fe7b0c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27ea65a-63dd-575c-8716-61bbc057cee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334417} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceac7b91-bf3d-5f08-a0dd-74c2347b792d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "525991ab-a7d5-5a16-ae31-a0c0ee48b9e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64253" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8R\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=8R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0572e053-bbb1-51d9-80e9-c92d6011545b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431121} {:answer/id #uuid "08d858c9-ca22-560b-97eb-40da3122196f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431122} {:answer/id #uuid "3dddd46e-c3a5-5c09-a4c6-1f8c377929ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431126} {:answer/id #uuid "90dfd4e1-ed47-5aa3-9478-4f0c76e8f799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431131} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fd2ae2-4033-52e2-b506-d30e73572664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0572e053-bbb1-51d9-80e9-c92d6011545b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431121} :answer-field/id #uuid "5544434a-2b77-5acf-bab7-2044c21c61df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1d55e9e-973d-5821-a2d2-76fe96bd1d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-64257" :question/problem "A particle of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=30\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+6y=0$\n\nII. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n\nIII. The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{30}{5}=6\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n1\\. $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n2\\. $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n3\\. $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{6}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 3 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f5a553a-2376-55cd-bc5e-82413407a146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399256} {:answer/id #uuid "0e31193a-a47b-5236-90bf-56a2004b1817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399257} {:answer/id #uuid "fd17c3ac-bd9b-5d08-b0bf-0332bb052907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399258} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f48a49-3778-5c56-a656-600e04741a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399259} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f6cb90-2038-5a8d-9cb0-03bdb5ac61c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+16{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f5a553a-2376-55cd-bc5e-82413407a146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399256} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30d8193-6f9e-52bd-a96a-3e323a570917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "873cc1aa-ff8b-506b-b81a-49f440b9d6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94267" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca25542-f23c-589a-a5dc-6123e88b0013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394972} {:answer/id #uuid "9b339dd2-12ba-5982-a340-48035cfccbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394973} {:answer/id #uuid "5362cf40-1431-547a-b9d6-c5c711bd3d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{144}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394974} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb7bb2d-1e07-50d5-9fce-e333ad3d4d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394975} {:answer/id #uuid "acff6b9d-3485-5b9e-ad96-66592c8b7049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cb7bb2d-1e07-50d5-9fce-e333ad3d4d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394975} :answer-field/id #uuid "46290546-4ff2-5d39-8b98-b300425eb07b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d55619d9-04e6-5e22-adf5-2ab8249416dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94274" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040b3a63-19be-5022-9658-3014fb50b885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354689} {:answer/id #uuid "84455952-9fa7-5e94-aa0f-d7d5bfb8e11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355830} {:answer/id #uuid "b15c462d-1b5e-57a3-adf5-fc61bd9e16cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356226} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5fd280-3e1b-5029-8757-e4ee17ca35c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356711} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4c80ad-01b9-599e-8626-a6256b045b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b15c462d-1b5e-57a3-adf5-fc61bd9e16cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356226} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbf2f3b-088a-5dda-9944-62e1f201bf5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b779ea2-64c9-5921-9b3e-0c6d8313380f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94275" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cf31f8d-0b9b-54c4-9952-e5cce2f4eb4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431128} {:answer/id #uuid "8411bef0-d8a8-527d-a004-2dd2d0cf6447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431129} {:answer/id #uuid "d83fc18f-3328-5871-b2d1-03511b17539e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431136} {:answer/id #uuid "ec54620d-42a6-5d21-824a-bb0be4ebb61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431138} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa1a9f9-35e4-54a6-80a6-ff1b93c42bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf31f8d-0b9b-54c4-9952-e5cce2f4eb4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431128} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf16abc1-c544-5419-8b7c-43b624566e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3951f87d-bb0f-5303-830e-d70693674542" :question/math-academy-id "q-94279" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nTherefore, only statement III could be used to represent  $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2069605a-a81e-57fb-a295-96c83fa94991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431124} {:answer/id #uuid "2c726ff3-14e5-5477-aa74-d916199a65c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431125} {:answer/id #uuid "885afa26-b165-57dd-aac5-64a8d6e83458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431130} {:answer/id #uuid "c53a8eda-0954-5e4e-9d01-ec5263955f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431133} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fde5da-39ec-5034-b103-3464e9114e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "885afa26-b165-57dd-aac5-64a8d6e83458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431130} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c9dae89-1437-5756-92da-8bdbfec6dd5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58a73fa1-b7ae-5890-859a-ca230c9a8f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94282" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4R\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=4R\\,⟹\\,R=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $2\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ec6562-d48f-5a6a-8660-68e1c852de8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355241} {:answer/id #uuid "616d5349-0070-55c4-b7ca-036db233223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355530} {:answer/id #uuid "c816f4e0-9064-5352-ac0e-4e0580554bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356416} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ad12a4-e0d5-5774-a055-04562ef23b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356422} {:answer/id #uuid "d45eee0e-5286-5bae-9aad-b285be2058b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ec6562-d48f-5a6a-8660-68e1c852de8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355241} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bf3659f-43c3-5fd3-9c11-b1a1a81c6eb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba39584f-74b3-570c-9def-e711c021e6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94285" :question/problem "A particle of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=4\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$"}]}
